#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:157
msgid "Sentence-Level Clarity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:159
msgid "From Gopen & Swan: \"The Science of Scientific Writing\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:161
msgid ""
"The seminal 1990 paper by George Gopen and Judith Swan establishes that "
"**readers have structural expectations** about where information appears "
"in prose. Violating these expectations forces readers to spend energy on "
"structure rather than content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:163
msgid ""
"\"If the reader is to grasp what the writer means, the writer must "
"understand what the reader needs.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:165
msgid "The 7 Principles of Reader Expectations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:167
msgid "**Principle 1: Subject-Verb Proximity**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:169
msgid ""
"Keep grammatical subject and verb close together. Anything intervening "
"reads as interruption of lesser importance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:171
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"The model, which was trained on 100M tokens and fine-tuned on"
" domain-specific data using LoRA with rank 16, achieves state-of-the-art "
"results\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:173
msgid ""
"**Strong**: \"The model achieves state-of-the-art results after training "
"on 100M tokens and fine-tuning with LoRA (rank 16)\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:175
msgid "**Principle 2: Stress Position (Save the Best for Last)**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:177
msgid ""
"Readers naturally emphasize the **last words of a sentence**. Place your "
"most important information there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:179
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"Accuracy improves by 15% when using attention\" **Strong**: "
"\"When using attention, accuracy improves by **15%**\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:182
msgid "**Principle 3: Topic Position (First Things First)**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:184
msgid ""
"The beginning of a sentence establishes perspective. Put the \"whose "
"story\" element first—readers expect the sentence to be about whoever "
"shows up first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:186
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"A novel attention mechanism that computes alignment scores is"
" introduced\" **Strong**: \"To address the alignment problem, we "
"introduce a novel attention mechanism\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:189
msgid "**Principle 4: Old Information Before New**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:191
msgid ""
"Put familiar information (old) in the topic position for backward "
"linkage; put new information in the stress position for emphasis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:193
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"Sparse attention was introduced by Child et al. The quadratic"
" complexity of standard attention motivates this work.\" **Strong**: "
"\"Standard attention has quadratic complexity. To address this, Child et "
"al. introduced sparse attention.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:196
msgid "**Principle 5: One Unit, One Function**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:198
msgid ""
"Each unit of discourse (sentence, paragraph, section) should serve a "
"single function. If you have two points, use two units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:200
msgid "**Principle 6: Articulate Action in the Verb**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:202
msgid "Express the action of each sentence in its verb, not in nominalized nouns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:204
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"We performed an analysis of the results\" (nominalization) "
"**Strong**: \"We analyzed the results\" (action in verb)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:207
msgid "**Principle 7: Context Before New Information**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:209
msgid ""
"Provide context before asking the reader to consider anything new. This "
"applies at all levels—sentence, paragraph, section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/references_writing-guide.md:211
msgid ""
"**Weak**: \"Equation 3 shows that convergence is guaranteed when the "
"learning rate satisfies...\" **Strong**: \"For convergence to be "
"guaranteed, the learning rate must satisfy the condition in Equation "
"3...\""
msgstr ""

